Here\'92s an analysis of how the application is designed to handle multiple users.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 ## How the Application Handles Multiple Users\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
The application is built on a standard, scalable web architecture that handles multiple users concurrently and efficiently.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Frontend (Each User's Browser):
\f0\b0  Each user who visits the URL gets their own independent copy of the React user interface running in their web browser. All UI-specific data, like the "Query History," is stored locally for that user and is completely separate from other users.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Backend (Hugging Face Space):
\f0\b0  Your FastAPI application runs on a Gunicorn web server, which is designed for concurrency. Gunicorn starts and manages multiple "worker" processes. When requests come in from different users, Gunicorn acts as a traffic controller, distributing the work across these workers so that multiple queries can be processed in parallel.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 External Services (Database & LLMs):
\f0\b0  The Supabase database and the Google/Groq LLM APIs are powerful cloud services built to handle thousands of simultaneous requests. They are not a bottleneck.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 ## How Resources are Shared (Duplication)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
There is very little duplication on the server, as resources are shared efficiently. A new copy of the application is 
\f1\b not
\f0\b0  created for each user.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Shared Server Processes:
\f0\b0  All users connect to the same running Gunicorn server on your Hugging Face Space.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Shared In-Memory Models:
\f0\b0  The most memory-intensive parts of your application\'97the Gemini and Groq clients, the SQL agent, and the sentence-transformer embeddings model\'97are loaded into memory only once per worker process. The 
\f2\fs26 _initialize_services
\f0\fs24  function in your code is designed to check if a service already exists before creating a new one. This is a highly efficient "singleton" pattern that ensures these large models are reused for all subsequent user queries, which saves memory and improves response time.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 The only "duplication" that occurs is that each user's browser downloads a copy of the frontend code, which is how all modern websites function. In summary, your application is architected correctly to serve multiple users efficiently without duplicating backend resources.\
}